  "id": "COMPOUND:14531 - PROTEIN:5777",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that neomycin may interact with SF-188 through multiple distinct pathways, all leading through the hepatocyte as a common intermediate. Path_181387 links neomycin to HuT78, which is targeted by Methylseleno carboxyethylglutamine, and then to SF-188. Path_181388 and Path_181389 both connect neomycin to hepatocytes, which are targeted by 7-O-Methylmangiferin and Camelliquercetiside B, respectively, which also target SF-188. These findings indicate that neomycin may act as a modulator of multiple pathways that ultimately converge on SF-188, suggesting a potential role in regulating cellular processes through these intermediaries.\n\nThe mechanisms appear to involve the targeting of hepatocytes by various compounds, which in turn may influence SF-188 through different molecular interactions. The presence of multiple distinct targets (Methylseleno carboxyethylglutamine, 7-O-Methylmangiferin, and Camelliquercetiside B) suggests that neomycin may exert its effects through diverse biochemical pathways, potentially involving different cellular processes. The integration of these pathways implies that neomycin could modulate SF-188 through a complex network of interactions, possibly involving metabolic or signaling pathways that are not yet fully characterized. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between neomycin and SF-188, but further investigation is needed to elucidate the precise molecular mechanisms involved.",
